The alarm goes off at five and the cottage is still dark. This is not the kind of waking that requires convincing — you remember, the moment your feet hit the floor, that there are crab pots on the dock. Everything else can wait.

Hull Creek runs quiet at that hour. The water is flat and barely distinguishable from the sky, both of them the same gray-blue that exists only in the twenty minutes before real light arrives. Osprey have already started working somewhere upstream — you can hear them before you see them, that sharp descending cry carrying across the still surface. {/* TODO: Will to confirm — are osprey audible from the dock before dawn in summer, or does this need to be softened to "by full light"? Guest review (Maya, Brooklyn) confirms osprey on the creek; specific timing unconfirmed by primary source. */}

The dock planks are wet with dew. You don't need a flashlight, but you move carefully.

## What the Tide Tells You

Blue crabs feed on tidal movement, and Hull Creek is a tidal system — a 6.5-mile tributary of the Potomac, fully influenced by the rhythms of the lower river and the bay beyond it. The practical upshot is this: set your pots in the evening, around high tide, and pull them at first light after the water has turned and the crabs have been working through the night. That's the sequence the dock is designed for.

{/* TODO: Will to confirm — is the recommended set/pull timing "evening then dawn" specifically, or does it vary by season? The evening-set/dawn-pull approach is consistent with Chesapeake watermen practice and the site's own copy ("run lines from the dock, pull at dawn"), but Will may want to add nuance here. */}

The Northern Neck has a longer relationship with crab pots than almost anywhere else. A Northumberland County waterman named Benjamin Franklin Lewis invented the wire crab pot in 1938 on the Yeocomico River, just over the county line — and the commercial pot used on the Chesapeake and Potomac today is essentially unchanged from his design. When you lower one off this dock, you are using the same geometry those watermen have relied on for nearly ninety years. That is a strange and satisfying continuity.

## Baiting and Dropping

{/* TODO: Will to confirm — exact number and type of pots at the dock. Site copy says "crab pots" (plural); this post uses "a few" deliberately to avoid stating a specific number until confirmed. Also confirm: wire trap or crab ring/line? Technique details below assume wire trap pots (enclosed trap), which aligns with the Lewis patent heritage, but this needs confirmation. */}

The cottage keeps {/* TODO: Will to confirm — how many? */} crab pots ready at the dock. Gear storage is {/* TODO: Will to confirm — dock box, storage bin, covered shelf? */} nearby. You'll want bait: chicken necks are the traditional Chesapeake choice, reliably available at the grocery store in Heathsville. <!-- TODO: Will to confirm — can you verify chicken necks are consistently stocked there, or is there a closer bait option? --> They're durable enough to last multiple pulls and attract blue crabs reliably; if you want a faster scent trail, oily fish — menhaden if you can find it — will draw crabs more quickly but won't hold up as long.

Bait the pot, clip it closed, attach a float line, and lower it off the edge of the dock into the deeper channel. <!-- TODO: Will to confirm — is there a defined deeper channel accessible from the dock, or do pots go in the shallower brackish water? Site copy notes the water is "shallow, mostly fresh" — clarify where the pots are most productive. --> The work is slower than it sounds, and by the time you've dropped the last one, you'll have been standing in the dark long enough to notice the sky shifting.

This is when you go inside and make coffee.

## The Wait

The screened porch faces the creek. In the hour between dropping the pots and the light being bright enough to see what's in them, the morning does its work without you. The fog that sits on the water in summer burns off slowly. Herons move through the shallows with the patience of professionals. <!-- TODO: Will to confirm — herons on the creek in summer mornings? Ecologically consistent with tidal creek habitat; not confirmed by a named primary source. Osprey confirmed by guest review (Maya). -->

*Hull Creek* is not a place that rewards urgency. This is in its nature — a tributary creek on the <a href={withBase("/area")}>Northern Neck</a>, tucked into Northumberland County, where the Potomac is broad and slow and things have been done at a waterman's pace for generations. The wait is not dead time. It is part of the morning.

## Pulling and Culling

An hour after high tide, or at first light if you set the evening before, is when you pull. <!-- TODO: Will to confirm — preferred pull timing from the dock; this is general guidance, not specific to Hull Creek conditions. --> Walk the dock, haul the line hand over hand, and set the pot on the planks.

What comes up will vary. Here is the simple sorting rule:

**Males (jimmies)** have blue-tipped claws and a long, pointed apron on their underside — shaped roughly like the Washington Monument. Any jimmy measuring five inches or better, point-to-point across the widest part of the shell, is a keeper under Virginia regulations.

**Mature females (sooks)** have orange or red-tipped claws and a rounded, dome-shaped apron. Virginia has no minimum size for mature females — if a crab has a sook's apron and red-orange claws, she can come home with you.

**Immature females** — look for the narrow, unformed apron — go back. They're not done yet.

A five-inch male is not large. Hold one up against your palm and you'll have a sense for the threshold quickly. The first time, measure; after a few mornings, you develop an eye.

The <a href={withBase("/activities")}>activities page</a> has more on what the dock makes possible — the kayaks and paddleboard that let you work the creek's shallower edges, and the other gear the cottage keeps available by season.

**On regulations:** Virginia's recreational crabbing rules allow up to two pots per person without a license through the season (March 17–December 20). If your group wants to run more pots, a license is required; fees and specific pot limits vary and can change year to year. Check the current rules at [mrc.virginia.gov](https://mrc.virginia.gov/regulations/VA-recreational-crabbing-rules.shtm) before your stay — it's a quick read and worth having current.

## Steaming on the Screened Porch

<!-- TODO: Will to confirm — steaming setup at the cottage. Specifically: outdoor propane burner/crab cooker, or stovetop only? What pot(s) are available? The "screened porch" beat is in the brief's intended narrative arc, but the Writer cannot confirm the porch is the actual cooking location without knowing the setup. This paragraph is written to be cooking-location-agnostic; Will should add the specific setup detail. -->

By the time you've culled the morning's haul, the light is fully up and the day has technically begun, though it doesn't feel that way — it feels like you've already had a whole separate morning before the one most people are waking into.

The steaming is simple: water in the pot, Old Bay, a rack to keep the crabs above the liquid, lid on, ten to fifteen minutes. The smell of Old Bay and steam is one of those smells that is identical to summer itself in this part of the world. When the shells go red, breakfast is ready.

